=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A complete multi-layer sweep of the ospf_enterprise_static topology found no fault, no mechanism, and no historical trace that could explain the reported "core link bouncing." All three core routers (router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3) are currently healthy across every layer examined.

**Affected component:** None — all subsystems are clean. No interface is DOWN or detached; no flap script or bounce process is running on any device; no tc qdisc is shaping or corrupting traffic on any interface.

**Mechanism (absence):** A link-flap fault leaves characteristic traces even when temporarily quiescent: an active flap process visible in `pgrep`, non-startup carrier-change counters in `/proc/net/dev` or ethtool, and OSPF adjacency Down/Up events in the FRR ospfd log. None of these traces are present. The only dmesg bridge-state events observed are identical across all containers, confirming they are shared host-kernel STP initialization messages at lab boot — normal Kathara baseline behavior, not per-router carrier events.

**Localization:** The FRR logs on all three core routers contain only configuration-read startup messages (12:03–12:04 UTC). No subsequent adjacency-Down, ExStart, or Init state transitions appear. All OSPF neighbors are Full/DR or Full/Backup. All core-interconnect interfaces (eth0, eth1) are UP with zero error/drop counters.

**Root cause:** The intermittent link-flapping symptom reported in helpdesk ticket #8818 is unconfirmed — no injected fault, background process, or historical trace supports it in the current lab state.

**Fix:** No corrective action required. Monitor the lab under load for repeated OSPF adjacency events; if flapping recurs, capture `show ip ospf neighbor` and ospfd logs during an active episode.
=== END ===